  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Bio-realistic Model of a Mouse V1 Cortical Column\n",
    "\n",
    "This tutorial will run through the steps to run the Allen Institute V1 cortical column model from Billeh et al. 2020. The model consists of two main components - filterNet, a model of LGN that behaves like a spatiotemporal filter and proccesses visual stimuli to provide input to V1, and the pointNet model of the V1 cortical column. PointNet models each neuron as simplified single compartment, with a small number of parameters, which greatly reduces simulation time while maintaining similar performance results compared to more detailed models (see [Teeter et al., 2018](https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-017-02717-4) for details on the GLIF models used here, and [Billeh et al., 2020](https://www.cell.com/neuron/fulltext/S0896-6273(20)30067-2?dgcid=raven_jbs_etoc_email) for comparison of PointNet with a more detailed version of the V1 cortical column model).\n",
    "\n",
    "This tutorial will cover the following topics:\n",
    "\n",
    "- How to set up and run the LGN and V1 models\n",
    "- How to provide different visual stimuli to the models\n",
    "- How to visualize and analyze the simulation results\n",
    "- How to set up a large batch of simulations efficiently\n",
    "\n",
    "This main tutorial requires a larger amount of compute resources, so we will switch from AWS to NSF compute nodes."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": null,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "Requesting runtime=hpc_cpu...\n",
      "[AnvilCPU:shared, 60 Minutes, 1 Node(s), 4 CPU(s), 0 GPU(s), 0 MB RAM, 1024 MB VRAM]\n",
      "* modules=[]\n",
